# Business Rules — Confirmed and Final

These rules were confirmed directly against the staffing agency's real business
data after multiple rounds of correction. Do NOT reinterpret, "simplify," or change
these formulas without an explicit, direct instruction from the user that includes
new worked examples. If asked to modify them, always re-verify against the worked
values below before shipping.

## Absence penalty formula (CONFIRMED FINAL)

```
Days 1–2:  free, 0 penalty
Days 3–7:  25 AED per absent day, counted cumulatively
Days 8+:   50 AED per absent day beyond day 7, ADDED on top of the
           125 AED already accrued from days 3–7
```

Formula: `penalty = min(max(days - 2, 0), 5) * 25 + max(days - 7, 0) * 50`

Required worked values — write a unit test asserting every one of these before
this logic ships:

| Absent days | Penalty (AED) |
|---|---|
| 0 | 0 |
| 1 | 0 |
| 2 | 0 |
| 3 | 25 |
| 4 | 50 |
| 5 | 75 |
| 6 | 100 |
| 7 | 125 |
| 8 | 175 |
| 9 | 225 |
| 10 | 275 |

This must be implemented as a configurable rule (a rates/rules table with tier
boundaries as data), not a hardcoded function — so tier boundaries can be adjusted
by an admin without a redeploy. But the DEFAULT/current values must match the table
above exactly.

## Bonus rule (CONFIRMED FINAL)

Flat 50 AED bonus if `days_absent = 0` for the timesheet's period. No proration —
even a single absent day forfeits the entire bonus, not a reduced amount.

## Salary floor — never negative (CONFIRMED FINAL)

```
gross_salary = hours_worked * pay_rate_aed_per_hour
net_salary = max(0, gross_salary - absence_penalty_amount + bonus_amount)
```

If `absence_penalty_amount > gross_salary`, do NOT silently absorb the shortfall.
Instead:
- Set `needs_attention = true`
- Set `attention_reason = 'penalty_exceeds_gross'`
- Block the timesheet from being submitted/approved until a human resolves it

Whether the unrecovered shortfall is forgiven or carried forward to the next pay
period is an OPEN BUSINESS POLICY QUESTION for the agency, not a technical default.
Build the schema to support a `carried_forward_amount` field on the next period's
timesheet regardless of which policy the agency picks, so this isn't a rebuild
later.

## Client billing rate resolution (CONFIRMED FINAL)

- Never guess or default a `client_charge_rate_aed_per_hour`. If no matching
  `client_rate_rules` row exists for a worker's (client, trade) pair, leave
  `client_charge_amount` and `profit_amount` NULL.
- Set the appropriate `attention_reason`:
  - `missing_charge_rate` — no rate exists anywhere for this client/trade
  - `charge_rate_ambiguous` — multiple candidate rates exist and none uniquely match
  - `missing_trade_for_charge_rate` — worker/timesheet has no trade assigned, so no
    rate lookup is possible at all
- A single trade having different rates across different clients is NORMAL and
  expected (e.g., Helper = 7 AED at MCC vs 8 AED at AL TAWASUL). This is not itself
  an error condition — it only becomes `charge_rate_ambiguous` when the system can't
  determine which specific client/trade combination applies to a given row.

## Worker identity resolution (CONFIRMED FINAL)

- Match incoming timesheet rows to workers by `worker_code` first, always.
- Only fall back to fuzzy name matching via `worker_name_aliases` when no code is
  present in the source file, and require human confirmation before trusting a new
  alias for future automatic matching.